# R Code Line Wrapping Rules

This document outlines the rules for line wrapping in R code.

## Basic Rules

1. Use 4 spaces for indentation (not tabs)
2. Place commas at the end of lines (not the beginning)
3. Align continued lines with 4-space indentation
4. Place closing parentheses on their own line, aligned with the opening line

## Examples

### Function Calls

```R
# Good
result <- function_name(arg1 = value1,
    arg2 = value2,
    arg3 = value3
)

# Bad (vertical alignment)
result <- function_name(arg1 = value1
                      , arg2 = value2
                      , arg3 = value3
                      )

# Bad (no commas)
result <- function_name(arg1 = value1
    arg2 = value2
    arg3 = value3
)
```

### Data Frames

```R
# Good
df <- data.frame(col1 = values1,
    col2 = values2,
    col3 = values3,
    stringsAsFactors = FALSE
)

# Bad (vertical alignment)
df <- data.frame(col1 = values1
               , col2 = values2
               , col3 = values3
               , stringsAsFactors = FALSE
               )
```

### Lists

```R
# Good
my_list <- list(item1 = value1,
    item2 = value2,
    nested_list = list(subitem1 = subvalue1,
        subitem2 = subvalue2
    )
)

# Bad (vertical alignment)
my_list <- list(item1 = value1
              , item2 = value2
              , nested_list = list(subitem1 = subvalue1
                                , subitem2 = subvalue2
                                )
              )
```

### Control Structures

```R
# Good
if (condition) {
    result <- tryCatch(expression,
        warning = function(w) handle_warning(w),
        error = function(e) handle_error(e)
    )
}

# Bad (vertical alignment)
if (condition) {
    result <- tryCatch(expression
                     , warning = function(w) handle_warning(w)
                     , error = function(e) handle_error(e)
                     )
}
```

## Implementation

These rules can be implemented using the following patterns:

1. For function calls:
   - Start with the function name and opening parenthesis
   - Each argument on its own line with 4-space indentation
   - Comma at the end of each line (except the last)
   - Closing parenthesis on its own line, aligned with the function name

2. For data structures (data.frame, list):
   - Start with the structure name and opening parenthesis
   - Each element on its own line with 4-space indentation
   - Comma at the end of each line (except the last)
   - Closing parenthesis on its own line, aligned with the structure name

3. For control structures:
   - Start with the control keyword and opening parenthesis
   - Each argument on its own line with 4-space indentation
   - Comma at the end of each line (except the last)
   - Closing parenthesis on its own line, aligned with the control keyword

## Exceptions

1. Short function calls with few arguments can stay on one line
2. Simple assignments can stay on one line
3. Very short lists or data frames can stay on one line